WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.320
We've all had tough days at the office.

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:06.560
But some actors, dedicated to giving it their
all, took their work a few steps too far.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.200
Though these critically-acclaimed, often award-winning
performances are a delight to watch, the experience

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.390
of preparing for and recovering from these
roles did a number on the actors involved,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.960
and left a lasting impression — not always
for the positive.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.880
Here are several actors who went the extra
mile for their craft.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.280
Isabelle Adjani - Possession

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.360
Any horror buff knows the subway scene in
Andrzej Zulawski's 1981 Possession to be one

00:00:28.369 --> 00:00:32.730
of the most horrifying, shocking moments of
body horror ever to grace the silver screen.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.460
And if you haven't seen the film, buckle up,
you're in for a treat.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.320
Isabelle Adjani won a César Award for her
performance, but the intense physical and

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.950
emotional demands of the role made for an
extremely difficult recuperation.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:47.000
Adjani later told a French magazine that it
took her "years of therapy" to get Anna out

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.780
of her system, and that she would never again
attempt another role like it.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:51.860
Adrien Brody - The Pianist

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.250
Though Brody's physical transformation for
2002's The Pianist is obvious, the actor has

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:01.379
also discussed the enormous mental and emotional
strain of portraying a Holocaust survivor,

00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:04.100
which earned him the Academy Award for Best
Actor in 2003.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:11.790
"Y'know, my experiences of making this film
made me very aware of the sadness and dehumanization

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.100
of people at times of war."

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.020
To prepare for the role, Brody gave up his
apartment, sold his car, disconnected his

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.530
phones, and moved to Europe.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:22.070
But it was the emotional effect of intense
hunger during his extreme crash diet that

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.780
he found the most surprising and difficult
challenge to deal with.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:26.060
Brody told the BBC,

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.970
"I've experienced loss, I've experienced sadness
in my life, but I didn't know the desperation

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:31.970
that comes with hunger."

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:35.660
There were moments when he wasn't sure he'd
get out of the experience with his sanity

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.140
intact, saying it took a year and a half to,
quote, "settle back into things."

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.380
Colin Firth - The King's Speech

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:45.800
Colin Firth plays the future King of England,
George the Sixth, in the Oscar-winning The

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.270
King's Speech, and has to give a number of
speeches in addition to a number of other

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:49.840
royal duties.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.920
The film has a villain of sorts in the form
of a near-debilitating stammer that ruins

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.840
nearly every speech George gives until he
hires a vocal coach.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:02.440
"Prince Albert … Frederick … Arthur … George."

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.460
Firth himself also worked closely with a voice
coach and watched recordings of George speaking

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.860
to better emulate both his vocal shortcomings,
as well as his physical mannerisms and nervous

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.000
tics when stammering.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.700
Firth immersed himself so deeply in the role
that he admitted in an interview that he still

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:19.450
occasionally lapsed into the stammer when
speaking casually, even briefly stammering

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.460
during the interview itself.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.920
It's worth noting that this happened in May
of 2011, a full eight months after the film

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:26.810
premiered in September of the previous year.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:30.950
Judging by how flawlessly he enunciated every
syllable in Kingsman: The Secret Service while

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:35.320
taking down thugs with an umbrella in 2014,
it looks like Firth has since gotten over

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:35.820
the stammer.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:37.760
Hugh Laurie - House

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.950
During the casting process of House, the producers
famously explained that they wanted a "quintessentially

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:47.720
American actor" to play Dr. House, shortly
before hiring British actor Hugh Laurie.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:50.760
Laurie apparently got the role because his
American accent on his audition tape was so

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.510
convincing nobody realized he was British
— the director of the pilot even pointed

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.840
to the tape and said, "See, this is what I
want: an American guy."

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.140
"I was expecting you in my office 20 minutes
ago."

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.720
"Really?

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.100
That's odd, because I had no intention of
being in your office 20 minutes ago."

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.880
Laurie also really went for it when it came
to walking with a limp to portray the cane-using

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:09.380
House.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:13.400
So much so, the actor still walked with a
limp in 2015 after eight straight years of

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:14.870
pretending to have one on set.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.090
Laurie also reportedly attempted to ease the
load on his leg by occasionally switching

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:22.310
the leg had a limp, something he claims nobody
ever noticed or called him on during filming

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.520
or in the years since show ended.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:27.940
Apparently Laurie's acting is so good he can
make people overlook both his British-ness

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.900
and the fact he didn't always limp with the
same leg, despite that being a defining aspect

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:32.540
of the character.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.280
Bob Hoskins - Who Framed Roger Rabbit

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.840
One of late British actor Bob Hoskins' most
famous roles was that of alcoholic L.A. gumshoe

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.320
Eddie Valiant in the film Who Framed Roger
Rabbit.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.340
Hoskins had to spend several hours a day for
eight months talking to and acting alongside

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.340
cartoon characters who weren't really there.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.970
"Scotch on the rocks.

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.380
And I mean ice!"

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.240
Hoskins would later note that he "learned
how to hallucinate" that Roger and the other

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.850
characters were actually there to cope with
the dissonance of constantly hearing their

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.580
voices but never seeing them while filming.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.320
When shooting finally ended, Hoskins found
himself constantly talking to himself and

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:09.110
even hallucinating that Roger was sitting
in the same room for months afterwards, prompting

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.940
his doctor to advise him to take a much needed
break from acting.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.020
Heath Ledger - The Dark Knight

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.040
Ledger's performance as the Joker in The Dark
Knight was so chilling that it landed him

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.410
an Oscar for Best Supporting Actor—tragically,
it was a posthumous award, as Ledger had died

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.760
of an accidental drug overdose in January
2008.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:33.240
"Does it depress you, commissioner, to know just
how alone you really are?"

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:37.240
In the years that followed his premature death,
rumors swirled that the preparation for the

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.340
dark role had contributed to Ledger's demise.

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:43.720
Before filming began, Ledger put himself in
strict isolation, keeping a diary of disturbing

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.740
images to enter "the realm of a psychopath."

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:49.900
He sometimes only slept "two hours a night,"
while filming, according to a November 2007

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.800
interview with The New York Times.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.960
It was a mixture of "painkillers, anti-anxiety
drugs and sleeping pills" that ultimately

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.920
caused Ledger's death just two months later.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.300
Charlie Hunnam - American TV &amp; Movies

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.730
Charlie Hunnam has played everything from
a soccer hooligan to a giant robot pilot,

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:08.720
and he's known for his extraordinarily convincing
American accent, making him one of a handful

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.300
of chameleon-like British actors able to convincingly
pass as American in his various roles.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.830
"You rescued her.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:16.160
You raised her.

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.900
You're not protecting her now.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.160
You are holding her back."

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:25.590
Years of living in the States, however, took
their toll on Hunnam's actual, real-life accent,

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:29.670
and when he appeared on TV in 2013 to plug
a movie on Conan, he spoke with a bizarre

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:34.090
amalgamation of various American dialects
that prompted confusion and mockery, especially

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:35.090
in Hunnam's native U.K.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:40.280
"I went out just as he was rounding the side
of the house, and he stopped, I looked at

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.740
him, and said, 'So we got business, motherf-----?'"

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:48.860
Hunnam spoke about this in 2017 when he admitted
that his accent—or lack thereof—had gotten

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:52.680
so bad that when he signed on to star in King
Arthur: Legend of the Sword, he had to hire

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.500
a dialect coach to re-learn how to speak with
an English accent.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.830
"For the first time in my career my name was
above the title.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.030
It said, 'Charlie Hunnam, BOOM, King Arthur.'

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:02.680
I said, 'Here we go.'"

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:05.120
Janet Leigh - Psycho

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:09.030
Academy Award-winning actress Janet Leigh
is known mainly for one role: playing the

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:13.380
character who gets stabbed to death in a shower
early on in Alfred Hitchcock's Psycho.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.979
The scene terrified millions, including Leigh—after
filming wrapped, she never felt comfortable

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:18.820
in a shower again.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:22.900
In interviews Leigh noted that she "stopped
taking showers" after watching the scene.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:26.620
"I still... I still don't take showers, that the truth."

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:30.220
On the rare occasion she had to take a shower,
she would only use it briefly while staring

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.180
directly at the door—and she wouldn't even
draw the shower curtain.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:34.960
James Cromwell - Babe

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:39.250
James Cromwell is the kind of actor whose
appearance is so distinctive most people know

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:40.669
his face, but not his name.

00:06:40.669 --> 00:06:45.170
He scored one of his few leading film roles
in 1995's Babe, in which he was upstaged by

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:48.630
a talking pig—but it all worked out, given
that it was not only a massive hit, but it

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:52.780
changed Cromwell's life, turning him from
a guy who occasionally flirted with vegetarianism

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:53.400
into a vegan.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.500
Cromwell has called the experience of making
the film a turning point in his life, recalling

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:01.199
in an interview with Vice that he was profoundly
affected by seeing a small piglet react to

00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:02.699
being put onto a patch of grass.

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:06.440
He said, quote, "When that little pig was
put down on that big pitch and saw the blue

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:09.650
sky and the green grass and the sea, that
pig just took off.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.620
I said, I don't want any part of this.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:12.120
I am out."

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:16.380
Cromwell has been an ardent supporter of animal
rights ever since—especially pigs, which,

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:20.120
understandably, now have a special place in
his heart thanks to Babe and its sequel, Babe

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:21.210
II: A Pig in the City.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:26.690
"I know that I'm aware of their suffering,
and I know I have an obligation to talk about

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:29.640
their suffering, and to do something about
their suffering."

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.480
Christopher McDonald - Happy Gilmore

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.880
Christopher McDonald isn't exactly a household
name, but his performance in Happy Gilmore

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:40.199
is so memorable that generations of filmgoers
can't look at a picture of his face without

00:07:40.199 --> 00:07:42.449
blurting out "Hey, it's Shooter McGavin!"

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:45.630
That's just one role out of many in a solid
career, but it looks like McDonald doesn't

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:48.639
mind being forever associated with the hot-headed
golf pro.

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:49.639
McDonald told the A.V.

00:07:49.639 --> 00:07:53.139
Club he took the role basically because he
enjoyed playing golf—and won a tournament

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:54.830
shortly after being offered the script.

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:58.669
He was hesitant at first, saying he wasn't
eager to get back on a film set after shooting

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:02.570
two movies back to back, but being paid to
play golf and hang out with Adam Sandler seemed

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:03.380
like a pretty sweet deal.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:07.380
According to McDonald, his "golf game got
sick" since he played for five hours a day,

00:08:07.389 --> 00:08:11.539
six days a week while filming, and as an added
bonus, now that he's synonymous with Shooter

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:14.700
McGavin, he basically gets to play golf for
free for the rest of his life.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.140
"Damn you people!

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:20.080
This is golf, not a rock concert!"

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.360
Thanks for watching!

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.520
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.669
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

